// Haptic feedback — the one output channel this app never used.
//
// Why it exists at all: the press-and-hold gesture has a 500ms threshold that
// the user cannot see. Before this, crossing it looked identical to not
// crossing it right up until a menu appeared, so the only way to learn the
// gesture was to hold too long on purpose. A tick at the threshold is the
// feedback the visual layer structurally cannot give — the finger is covering
// the thing that would have shown it.
//
// Deliberately two events, not a palette. Haptics stop carrying information the
// moment everything buzzes, so the rule here is: fire only where the user
// crossed a boundary they could not see, or where something failed and the
// visual signal is a small coloured toast that may be off-screen.
//
//   long-press  — a hold gesture just armed. The menu is about to open.
//   error       — an action failed. Pairs with an error toast, never alone.
//
// Anything the user can plainly see happen does not get a buzz.
//
// Wiring: Rust binds these in `crate::bin_ui::haptics` to the platform. On
// Android that is `View.performHapticFeedback`, which routes through the
// system's own haptics setting — a user who has turned touch feedback off in
// Android settings gets nothing, which is correct and is why this does not use
// the Vibrator service (that would override the preference and need the VIBRATE
// permission). On desktop nothing binds them and an unhandled Slint callback is
// a no-op, so this costs those builds a virtual call that returns.
export global Haptics {
    callback long-press();
    callback error();
}
